commit db7c39dabd6e0584e6dba801a901a2477492d2d4
Author: Daniel Drake <drake@endlessm.com>
Date:   Tue Jun 4 14:51:21 2019 +0800

    PCI: Add Intel remapped NVMe device support
    
    Consumer products that are configured by default to run the Intel SATA AHCI
    controller in "RAID" or "Intel RST Premium With Intel Optane System
    Acceleration" mode are becoming increasingly prevalent.
    
    Unde this mode, NVMe devices are remapped into the SATA device and become
    hidden from the PCI bus, which means that Linux users cannot access their
    storage devices unless they go into the firmware setup menu to revert back
    to AHCI mode - assuming such option is available. Lack of support for this
    mode is also causing complications for vendors who distribute Linux.
    
    Add support for the remapped NVMe mode by creating a virtual PCI bus,
    where the AHCI and NVMe devices are presented separately, allowing the
    ahci and nvme drivers to bind in the normal way.
    
    Unfortunately the NVMe device configuration space is inaccesible under
    this scheme, so we provide a fake one, and hope that no DeviceID-based
    quirks are needed. The interrupt is shared between the AHCI and NVMe
    devices.
    
    Allow pci_real_dma_dev() to traverse back to the real DMA device from
    the PCI devices created on our virtual bus, in case the iommu driver
    will be involved with data transfers here.
    
    The existing ahci driver is modified to not claim devices where remapped
    NVMe devices are present, allowing this new driver to step in.
    
    The details of the remapping scheme came from patches previously
    posted by Dan Williams and the resulting discussion.
    
    https://phabricator.endlessm.com/T24358
    https://phabricator.endlessm.com/T29119
    
    [ naim: intel-nvme-remap: Check for NULL on failed allocation ]
    
    Signed-off-by: Daniel Drake <drake@endlessm.com>
    Signed-off-by: Eric Naim <dnaim@cachyos.org>

commit 962bee17a9d7d5cd3369b70c0914fe050b66b948
Author: Peter Jung <admin@ptr1337.dev>
Date:   Mon Nov 2 11:31:47 2026 +0100

    rust: Allow BTF generation with Rust + LTO
    
    Commit 5daa0c35a1f0 ("rust: Disallow BTF generation with Rust + LTO")
    keeps RUST off when LTO and DEBUG_INFO_BTF are both enabled. Our default
    kernel is built with Clang ThinLTO and needs BTF for sched_ext and the
    BPF tooling, so olddefconfig silently drops RUST, and with it the Rust
    abstractions and the DRM panic QR code.
    
    With pahole 1.32, the BTF of a ThinLTO vmlinux with Rust built in is
    fine. Rust modules are what fails: when pahole merges the CUs of an LTO
    object, the merged CU takes the language of the first CU, which is the
    Rust one for Rust modules. --lang_exclude=rust then drops the whole
    merged CU, and gen-btf.sh fails:
    
      FAILED: load BTF from samples/rust/rust_minimal.ko.BTF.1: No such file or directory
    
    This is fixed in pahole by the series "dwarf_loader: Fix --lang_exclude
    on merged CUs", which is not in a release yet and does not bump the
    version, so the dependency cannot be gated on PAHOLE_VERSION. Drop the
    !LTO part of it and build with a pahole that carries these fixes.
    
    Tested with Clang 23 and rustc 1.99 on x86_64 ThinLTO with DWARF 5,
    DEBUG_INFO_BTF_MODULES and MODULE_ALLOW_BTF_MISMATCH=n: the vmlinux and
    C module BTF are the same as with stock pahole, the Rust sample modules
    and rnull get BTF and load, and scx_bpfland and scx_cosmos attach.
    
    Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261010005227.976-1-linux@alanderk.com/
    Signed-off-by: Peter Jung <admin@ptr1337.dev>

